WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:22.280
New Care Models – Frameworks for Primary and Acute Care Systems (PACS) and Enhanced Health in Care Homes (ECH)

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:29.200
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: At our July Board, we considered
the MCP care model, and today I bring the

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:36.180
equivalent frameworks for PACS and Enhanced
Health in Care Homes. These two frameworks

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:43.449
assemble together the constituent ingredients
of reform from the work of the vanguard systems,

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:49.829
and they now define the models. Nearly all
the STPs are planning to develop new models

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:56.539
of accountable care provision, whether PACS,
MCPs or a combination, along with the expansion

00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:03.429
of collaborations across acute services, and
so they will become core delivery mechanisms

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:10.750
for STPs. In some of the smaller STP footprints,
a PACS may cover the whole STP; for most,

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.190
more probably, they will form sub-geographies.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:22.110
Both PACS and MCPs involve the fundamental
redesign of care. They are about engaging

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:29.990
and activating patients and communities, harnessing
technology and making better use of the workforce.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:36.570
Their purpose is to dissolve the partitions
between primary, community, acute and social

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:44.120
care services, backed by new business models.
The PACS and MCP care delivery models are

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:51.550
almost identical; they differ mainly in the
breadth of services in the PACS, which includes

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:59.970
the direct delivery of core hospital services.
Both take the registered GP list as a starting

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:06.980
point, and neither can work without the full
engagement of primary care. At the same time,

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:12.860
the commissioning of a PACS necessarily involves
potential conflicts of interest, and through

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:18.460
the Commissioning Committee we are working
through what, if any, additional safeguards

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:24.800
may be required beyond those already set out
in our statutory guidance and the consultation

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.830
document that you have just alluded to.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:34.140
We are seeing some impressive individual examples
of change in the vanguards, that are beginning

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:40.780
to make a demonstrable difference in moderating
demand and improving the efficiency of supply:

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:46.780
in Mid-Nottinghamshire, the PRISM teams; the
integration of social care with acute services

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:53.120
in Salford; the emergency care redesign in
Northumbria; the management of high-need,

00:02:53.120 --> 00:03:00.640
high-cost patients in Yeovil. The task now
is the systematic implementation of all elements

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.790
of the framework by all of the PACS vanguards.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:11.730
Care redesign is the critical task, but it
does also need to be backed up by new commissioning

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:20.360
and contractual arrangements. As with MCPs,
we see three options: a virtual PACS, a partially

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:27.069
integrated model and a fully integrated model.
With NHSI, we have been developing a joint

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:33.790
assurance process which will consider both
the management of risk by the new providers,

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.950
as well as the effectiveness of the ongoing
commissioner function, and we will be taking

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:44.340
this through the next meeting of the Commissioning
Committee and seeking to publish that in October,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:51.319
as Simon referred to. It is worth noting that,
with the fully integrated commissioning model

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:58.050
for a PACS, it does imply a significant redrawing
of the boundary between what will continue

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:03.660
to be the commissioning function and what
is accountable provision.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:12.000
The mandate commits NHS England to achieving
at least 50% of the country having coverage

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:18.530
of new care models by 2020. As Simon mentioned,
we will be drawing up investment proposals

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:24.290
for spread from 2017/2018 in the light of
STP submissions.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:32.800
If I could now turn to enhanced health in
care homes. This is, I think, a much-neglected

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:39.530
area, and it has been fantastic to see the
levels of enthusiasm and engagement from the

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:47.900
NHS, local government, the voluntary sector
and the care home provision in trying to work

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:56.130
this through, improve the quality of life
for residents, support for staff in care homes,

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:01.780
as well as tackle some of the frustrations
that the NHS experiences as a result of the

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:08.900
interface not being well addressed. It is
a potential triple win there: for staff, for

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.830
patients and the NHS.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:18.380
The care home six, the vanguards, have been
developing and implementing the recipe of

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:25.440
what works, working this out with their local
partners and national bodies, and we are now

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.990
setting out the seven core elements and 18
constituent sub-elements, including a number

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:39.550
of low-cost interventions. We think the model
is clear, and many of the individual elements

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:47.729
are well known and based on pre-existing evidence,
professional best practice and NICE guidance.

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:52.340
Of the elements, I particularly highlight
the importance of the first, which is around

00:05:52.340 --> 00:06:00.920
achieving effective and enhanced primary care
support. We are seeing early results: reduction

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:07.620
in falls, lower prescribing costs, reduced
ambulance conveyances and fewer avoidable

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:16.199
admissions. The novelty of the care model
vanguards lies in their systematic implementation

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:22.020
of all the elements in combination, and thus
then to be able to understand what the full

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:30.110
effect of that will be. We see significant
interest across the country in adopting this

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:39.280
model, and scope for more rapid national roll-out
of this model given its narrower focus than

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:46.400
the whole-population models of MCPs and PACs,
and so later this year we are intending to

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:53.880
set out plans for national roll-out.
Chair, I commend both of these frameworks

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.860
to the Board.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.400
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: Obviously, new care models
are central to our success going forward,

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:09.370
and the care home work is excellent; it has
a number of really compelling points to it.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:15.061
My question is about the PACS: if I am an
average, everyday patient or citizen, what

00:07:15.061 --> 00:07:19.030
is it really going to mean for me? What is
going to be different, how am I going to feel

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:25.600
that the NHS is different?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I think at the heart of both

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:32.740
the MCPs and the PACS is really designing
the care around the needs of individual patients.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:40.720
So rather than you going to your GP for ten&nbsp;minutes,
irrespective of whether you have multiple

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:46.680
long‑term conditions or actually you are
normally well, what these models are doing

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:51.490
is, by looking at the totality of primary
and community services, and some hospital

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:56.690
services, they are designing what is really
needed for different groups of patients. This

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:02.169
means that, in somewhere like Yeovil, in the
frail elderly service, you have a comprehensive

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:07.750
assessment that is proactive; you have teams
of a geriatrician and the GP working with

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:14.840
the pharmacist, visiting you in your home
and providing much more intense follow‑up.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:20.229
It must make intuitive sense, where you have
high‑need, high‑cost patients, for the

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:30.789
NHS to put its efforts behind that.
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: For transparency, can I restate

00:08:30.789 --> 00:08:37.130
my declared interest as Chair of Skills for
Care, the workforce development agency for

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:44.810
the social care sector. I wanted particularly
to welcome the enhanced health in care homes

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:54.860
paper; it is great that the NHS is recognising
that residents of care homes have every right

00:08:54.860 --> 00:09:04.180
to the healthcare that any other citizen expects.
The advantages, as Ian said, for the NHS are

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:13.650
obvious. It leads me to the question of: why
have we not done it before? The answer is,

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:21.380
of course, the health and social care divide
that we all struggle with, and that it is

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:28.040
complex. It will not be easy to implement;
although it sounds deeply obvious, it will

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:37.270
not be easy to implement. I just wanted to,
in celebrating the achievement in the paper,

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:44.089
encourage colleagues to really emphasise that
this is not being done to the social care

00:09:44.089 --> 00:09:51.290
sector, but done with. Obviously, there is
a lot of work for the NHS to do, but it cannot

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:56.320
succeed if it is not done on a collaborative
basis. Sometimes the language and sometimes

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:04.810
the emphasis is very NHS-y, and not as collaborative
as it needs to be. So I wish it well, but

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:08.040
I think that that is an important point to
make.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:14.050
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Moira, I think you make an incredibly
important point. This will only work if it

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:21.000
is through an effective collaboration between
the health partners and the local authority

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:26.540
partners, and indeed the care homes providers
who have a critical role here in supporting

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:31.090
the delivery of the model.
On your first point as to why this has not

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:38.940
happened before: yes, it is complex, and it
will not be easy. However, my contention is

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:45.519
that one of the reasons why this has not happened
before is because of a lack of focus, and

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:51.640
we have not had the examples of people implementing
the different elements together and beginning

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:58.580
to understand the effects and the benefits
that can arise from that. I am optimistic

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:09.880
that we can actually roll this out in 2017/2018,
to do so at pace and at scale, and see some

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:15.309
of the benefits begin to accrue soon thereafter.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: The evidence does seem to

00:11:15.309 --> 00:11:20.820
quite clearly support the hypothesis on which
we launched the programme. I think it is very,

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:28.240
very strong evidence as to the impact.
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Ian, could you just talk

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:34.490
us through how you are going to scale and
replicate, and what some of the changes are

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:40.160
that you will face in doing so?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: For both of the models?

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.670
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Particularly for the
care homes work.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:53.530
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: There is a variety of different
mechanisms we are considering here. One of

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:59.870
them is to what extent we can encourage all
of the emerging models of accountable care

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:08.210
provision, to incorporate this as a core element.
We are seeing this already in Rushcliffe in

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:17.070
Nottingham, the Principia NCP is already adopting
this model. I think, bearing in mind the direction

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:25.470
of travel on STPs, that will help. I think
we can look at whether some of the elements,

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:33.769
or indeed the whole recipe, could be promulgated
across CCGs with the support of their partners

00:12:33.769 --> 00:12:40.200
more fully, and we are exploring that with
NHS Clinical Commissioners. I think, picking

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:48.300
up on Moira’s point, we can also enlist
the support of the care home provider sector,

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:54.960
given the importance of the training and the
upskilling of their staff, and we have interest

00:12:54.960 --> 00:13:02.170
from a number of the care home chains to participate
in those conversations and take an active

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:09.339
role in driving this forward.
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Also on the care homes initiative,

00:13:09.339 --> 00:13:13.440
Ian, just to remind you I am a consultant
in Newcastle Hospitals, which is the next‑door

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:20.670
neighbour of Gateshead, so I have a local
interest. You alluded to the improvement in

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:26.339
terms of reduced admissions to hospital: is
there a plan to actually document that in

00:13:26.339 --> 00:13:30.490
the vanguards and the roll‑out? Because
I think that would be a huge selling point

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:35.209
to the local health community, to actually
see that drop as opposed to anticipated or

00:13:35.209 --> 00:13:39.899
predicted.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Yes, exactly so. We have a combination

00:13:39.899 --> 00:13:44.480
of both local evaluation work and national
evaluation work that is looking at where the

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:49.920
different costs arise from implementing the
model, and the different benefits as well.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:57.200
In terms of the taxonomy of benefits, we think
that there will potentially be a one-off step

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:03.420
change, as well potential ongoing run-rate
reductions. Probably the easiest element to

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:13.480
get at is in avoidable prescribing costs.
We are also then seeing some reductions in

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:19.510
avoidable admissions and ambulance conveyances.
There is some variation between the different

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:25.740
six sites at the moment. That is partly to
do with the fact that they have not yet all

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:31.740
fully implemented all of the elements, and
have started from different starting points.

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:36.120
It is worth remembering that, when we are
thinking about wider spread, we will neither

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:41.720
incur the full costs of implementing all of
the elements from scratch nor indeed achieve

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:49.140
the full benefits, because this is not a greenfield
site; the NHS and the care home sector has

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:54.480
already been implementing different parts
of the model.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:15:06.100
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Ian, mine is on the PACS
model, and it is more about the balance between,

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:13.860
on the one hand, the need to continue the
joint working that exists between local GPs,

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:21.589
local health systems, trusts, etc.; and on
the other hand, the need for transparency

00:15:21.589 --> 00:15:29.410
and for confidence around procurement decisions.
That procurement decision picks up what Simon

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:34.090
said earlier, that some of these contracts
will be big and multi-year, and therefore

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:40.201
will require some of the insights that you
were talking about coming out earlier, with

00:15:40.201 --> 00:15:44.690
yourself and NHSI.
Also, there is a conflict of interest point,

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:52.019
and your paper alludes to it in 3.10, but
I think it is our duty to provide probably

00:15:52.019 --> 00:15:57.990
a bit more guidance than I think is implicit
in what is in your note; to help, as Malcolm

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:04.060
said before, to keep people safe as much as
for trying to get in the way of doing these

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:08.890
things. So I would be interested to know where
we are in that thinking. There is clearly

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:13.589
a tension and a trade-off, but I would be
interested to know how we are thinking about

00:16:13.589 --> 00:16:17.980
this and what we are intending to do.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: You raise a critically important

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:23.390
point, David, and it is one that our local
systems raised with us, because they would

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:29.160
like to see some national advice and guidance
on this to make their task easier. We are

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:39.260
working intensively with six systems on developing
the new contractual models for MCPs, but we

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:45.350
also have some of the PACS systems – Northumbria
and Yeovil most obviously – who have been

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:53.750
looking to go through significant procurements.
Our task here is to make sure that we can

00:16:53.750 --> 00:17:02.500
enable those systems to move at the pace they
want to go, so that is why we will have the

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:08.260
conversation at the next Commissioning Committee
on nailing down the best practical way of

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:15.629
doing this in terms of when people excuse
themselves from the conversations around procurement

00:17:15.629 --> 00:17:21.529
because they have an interest in being potentially
part of the provider, how that actually works

00:17:21.529 --> 00:17:26.269
through in practice, but in such a way that
does not harm the necessary collaboration

00:17:26.269 --> 00:17:33.960
between the GPs and the other potential providers.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think that is a very good

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:38.789
way of putting it. We are responsible for
putting these people into a situation where

00:17:38.789 --> 00:17:44.090
there is an institutionalised potential conflict
of interest. There is a good reason for doing

00:17:44.090 --> 00:17:48.029
that, but there is every good reason for not
going over the top and being punctilious.

00:17:48.029 --> 00:17:52.809
There is every good reason for having clear
principles, allowing people a safe area in

00:17:52.809 --> 00:17:58.429
which they can take decisions without threat
of a perceived conflict of interest or any

00:17:58.429 --> 00:18:02.899
other challenge.
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I was thinking about the

00:18:02.899 --> 00:18:09.139
role of the not-for-profit sector; I declared
my interest earlier, I run one. Particularly

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:12.980
in relation to people with complex needs – and
I should declare that I am the Chair of the

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:17.519
APPG on complex needs and dual diagnosis – I
am just wondering what your thoughts are around

00:18:17.519 --> 00:18:23.669
the engagement of service providers that are
going to be pretty critical to some of these

00:18:23.669 --> 00:18:29.419
complex needs, the notion of community-based
care and preventing people from going to hospital?

00:18:29.419 --> 00:18:36.029
I just wondered whether you could say a bit
more about concerns you have, or indeed notes

00:18:36.029 --> 00:18:43.200
for celebration, in terms of the PACS engagement
with the not-for-profit sector? And the ways

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:49.590
in, because it is a bit light on that.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Within the framework, we highlight

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:55.389
a few examples. I would particularly pick
up on your notes for celebration, Victor,

00:18:55.389 --> 00:19:04.639
because I have been very encouraged indeed
by the extent to which the PACS and indeed

00:19:04.639 --> 00:19:12.850
the MCPs have been thinking much more openly
than I have seen across, perhaps, typically

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:18.899
the rest of the NHS in how they work with
the voluntary sector and how they look at

00:19:18.899 --> 00:19:25.470
the role of carers, how do they change the
boundaries between formal care done to people

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:35.440
and informal care working with their local
communities? There are numerous examples in

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:45.999
mental health in Frimley Park, the engagement
of Age UK, the systematic creation of social

00:19:45.999 --> 00:19:52.760
prescribing models and connection – in a
really organised way – of lots of voluntary-sector

00:19:52.760 --> 00:20:00.479
partners through these models. I am sure we
can do more to bring those examples of best

00:20:00.479 --> 00:20:05.899
practice out more fully, and we are actually
working with the Academy of Fabulous Stuff

00:20:05.899 --> 00:20:13.590
in the next few weeks to try and highlight
some of those examples of good practice.

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:21.039
I think one of the elements of caution is
going to be with the wider roll-out at pace

00:20:21.039 --> 00:20:28.919
of some of these models. A lot of the STPs
are placing quite significant store on the

00:20:28.919 --> 00:20:33.970
importance of fundamental care redesign, and
there will be a challenge between the pace

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:38.999
at which they aspire to go and the need to
build those partnerships with their local

00:20:38.999 --> 00:20:43.979
communities. So I suspect this will be less
of an issue about the initial vanguard wave

00:20:43.979 --> 00:20:51.799
than, how do we really make sure that we embed
that mindset and those practices when we get

00:20:51.799 --> 00:20:58.049
to 50% of the country? I think your reminder
is a very timely and important one.

00:20:58.049 --> 00:21:03.570
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ian, very much
for the paper. I am going to invite the Board

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:09.519
to not only note the paper, but also to endorse
the framework documents that are part of this

00:21:09.519 --> 00:21:12.699
paper. Are we content to do that? Good, thank
you very much.

